A PN-Counter is just two G-Counters (per-replica maps of increments and decrements) merged independently by taking the max seen for each replica:

```python
class PNCounter:
    def __init__(self, replica_id):
        self.id = replica_id
        self.p = {}  # replica_id -> increments this replica has applied
        self.n = {}  # replica_id -> decrements this replica has applied

    def increment(self, amount=1):
        self.p[self.id] = self.p.get(self.id, 0) + amount

    def decrement(self, amount=1):
        self.n[self.id] = self.n.get(self.id, 0) + amount

    def value(self):
        return sum(self.p.values()) - sum(self.n.values())

    def merge(self, other):
        for rid, count in other.p.items():
            self.p[rid] = max(self.p.get(rid, 0), count)
        for rid, count in other.n.items():
            self.n[rid] = max(self.n.get(rid, 0), count)
```

Merging by `max` per replica is what makes this idempotent and order-independent — re-merging the same state twice, or merging in a different order, produces the same final counts, satisfying the CRDT guarantee.
